# Rust testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to Rust's built-in
`#[test]` framework and its common extensions. Applies only to `*.rs` files.

## Layout and naming

- Unit tests in a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { use super::*; ... }` block at the
  bottom of the file; integration tests under `tests/` at the crate root,
  one file per test binary.
- Name a test for the behavior it verifies (`rejects_negative_amount`, not
  `test1`).
- Fixture/golden data under `tests/fixtures/` (or `testdata/` if the project
  already uses it); match the crate's existing convention.

## Test structure

- A small table of cases iterated in one `#[test]` over many near-duplicate
  functions when cases share an assertion shape.
- `assert_eq!`/`assert!`/`assert_ne!` from `std`, or the project's adopted
  assertion crate (`pretty_assertions`, `assert_matches`); do not add a new
  one.
- For a `Result`-returning function, assert the specific error variant
  (`matches!(err, MyError::NotFound { .. })`, or `assert_eq!` when
  `PartialEq`), not only `result.is_err()`.

## Async tests

- `#[tokio::test]` (or the project's configured async test attribute) for
  async test bodies; no hand-rolled executor when the project depends on
  `tokio`.
- Join spawned tasks (`JoinHandle` `.await`, or a channel) before asserting;
  never `std::thread::sleep`/`tokio::time::sleep` to wait for a task.

## Property-based and fuzz testing

- `proptest` or `quickcheck` (whichever the crate depends on) for a large
  structured input space (a parser, a serializer round-trip).
- `cargo fuzz` (`libFuzzer`) for a parser or decoder of untrusted input;
  note in the report that it runs separately from `cargo test` and is not in
  the default CI run unless wired in.

## Benchmarks

- `criterion` for benchmarks needing statistical rigor, not the nightly-only
  built-in `#[bench]`.

## Running and determinism

- Run `cargo test` (add `--all-features`/`--workspace` when CI does) before
  reporting tests as passing; for wall-clock, real-network, or shared-global
  state use a fake clock, a mock boundary, or `#[serial]` (`serial_test`).
- New behavior gets a new test in the same change; a bug fix gets a
  regression test that fails before the fix.
